 $S$.\nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/606518c44f3238e9f489156e526e1479.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M$.\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M$.\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point because there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139534813 false] [17592186124109 1145 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <        \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <    \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/0af9e8b587bbeb640ff9ec0cdc8197a9d99e8f5282772561b92ba74915cf1226.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point because there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139534813 true] [17592186124110 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/38057eabc400c888e287be8dbbd8b4de172b625fa5922188f99f71112213ef3b.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty, 2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" 13194139534813 true] [17592186124110 1100 "Consider the set $[MATH: M=(-\\infty, 2]]$ and the points $A$, $B$, and $C$, shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/62389af9576e377c5c81474e27347e4f.png)\n\n1. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n2. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n3. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" 13194139534813 false] [17592186124110 1145 "Let $\\delta >0$. A *neighborhood* of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is the set of points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* $\\delta{:}$\n$\\{x: \\mid x- x_0 \\mid < \\delta \\}$\n\nA neighborhood of $x_0$ is given by the open interval $(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta)$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/87c93f9fa4ef79f3bf38fbcea72f758d.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $x_0$. The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S$.\n- A point $x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S$.\nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/22e313a10b473123c54290fd53624b0f.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/908766ecc45f4b98447e040aa693926d.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1930/Source/Images/5e9a6b515bc2690bb38cae29d12982e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" 13194139534813 false] [17592186124110 1145 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is the set of points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* $\\delta{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\{ x : |  x- x_0| <           \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of $x_0$ is given by the open interval $( x_0-\\delta,  x_0+\\delta),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/3e3aa5b8e0e93f7fda306a6f39c999af9a587614795316ce12fff8da2c9c2dd2.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/389aa307a3c0c10b5d51b86bed6e05331fb9299379652f06e65a4a7b9f776ead.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/1c0992788a0ac7ce8cab2caabb41479ba2267e532412d1ef2c7115f56afb911c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" 13194139534813 true] [17592186124117 1100 "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y > 4-2x\\big\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y = 4-2x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- The point $P(1, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139534813 true] [17592186124117 1100 "The set $A$ and its boundary 