ue] [17592186311739 1111 #uuid "4dcac0d7-86e8-54fc-b74e-154ba082ee60" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311740 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311741 13194139534813 true] [17592186311740 1089 "q-159550" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311740 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534813 true] [17592186311740 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/0e999290971eed02d7f12f9154bdb4d04f3328fd250b385059d94e83e3f195b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311740 1113 #uuid "c3bc4083-8c13-5c48-b54b-ebd4b788f065" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311740 1145 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/f10ff87f64dc07adb8e9535926186341bab88f2d0c783475ef8fd57b06b35fc0.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'." 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311742 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311743 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311744 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311745 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311746 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1041 "selection" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311742 13194139534813 true] [17592186311741 1110 #uuid "0cb983c5-93dd-5bf8-814f-37400052deb3" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311742 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311742 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534813 true] [17592186311742 1111 #uuid "23628713-1125-5b39-b629-3000ca7cc203" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311743 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534813 true] [17592186311743 1111 #uuid "8cd4b2c2-a345-52d2-9edc-aa2540003aa1" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311744 1034 "I only" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534813 true] [17592186311744 1111 #uuid "f989c9cd-9824-5e7f-acfa-a97c9204bdb6" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311745 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534813 true] [17592186311745 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534813 true] [17592186311745 